        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-cg-canonical-cell-exposed-faces-and-normal-cones",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(4) The face poset.** Every point of $Wx$ is a vertex of $P$; the faces of $P$ ([[def-extreme-point-and-face]]) are exactly the sets $\\operatorname{conv}(wW_Ux)$ for $w\\in W$ and $U\\subseteq S$, each occurring for exactly one coset $wW_U$; and $$\\operatorname{conv}(wW_Ux)\\subseteq\\operatorname{conv}(w'W_{U'}x)\\iff wW_U\\subseteq w'W_{U'}.$$ Hence $wW_U\\mapsto\\operatorname{conv}(wW_Ux)$ is an isomorphism from the coset poset ordered by reverse inclusion ([[def-cg-finite-reflection-arrangement-and-spherical-chambers]] (3)) onto the face poset of $P$ ordered by reverse inclusion, $\\dim\\operatorname{conv}(wW_Ux)=|U|$, and the setwise stabilizer of $\\operatorname{conv}(wW_Ux)$ in $W$ is $wW_Uw^{-1}$.\n\n**(5) Polyhedral cell description.** Put $c_s:=B(v_s,x)$ for $s\\in S$; then $c_s>0$, $0$ lies in the interior of $P$, and $$P=\\{v\\in V:B(wv_s,v)\\le c_s\\ \\text{for all }w\\in W,\\ s\\in S\\},$$ a compact convex polyhedral cell in the sense of [[def-finite-convex-cell-complex-and-linear-subdivision]] with $0$ in its interior.",
          "uses": [
