    "lem-cg-davis-cellulation-cw-structure-and-cayley-skeleta": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-cg-canonical-cell-exposed-faces-and-normal-cones",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(5) Polyhedral cell description.** Put $c_s:=B(v_s,x)$ for $s\\in S$; then $c_s>0$, $0$ lies in the interior of $P$, and $$P=\\{v\\in V:B(wv_s,v)\\le c_s\\ \\text{for all }w\\in W,\\ s\\in S\\},$$ a compact convex polyhedral cell in the sense of [[def-finite-convex-cell-complex-and-linear-subdivision]] with $0$ in its interior.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-cg-finite-coxeter-orbit-polytopes-and-face-metrics",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(1) Cells.** For every spherical $T$, $C_T$ is a compact convex polyhedral cell of dimension $|T|$ inside the Euclidean affine space $(V_T,B_T)$ with $0$ in its interior, a compact convex polyhedral cell in the sense of [[def-finite-convex-cell-complex-and-linear-subdivision]], and its faces are exactly the sets $\\operatorname{conv}(uW_Ux_T)$, $u\\in W_T$, $U\\subseteq T$, each occurring for exactly one coset $uW_U$; face inclusion agrees with coset inclusion (and the simultaneously reversed face and coset orders also agree). This is [[lem-cg-canonical-cell-exposed-faces-and-normal-cones]] applied to the finite-type system $(W_T,T)$ and the point $x_T$ in the open fundamental chamber $\\{v\\in V_T:B(v,e_s)>0\\text{ for all }s\\in T\\}$, whose distances to the simple mirrors are $B(x_T,e_s)=d_s$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-cg-davis-complex-cell-incidence-and-stabilizers",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "and the canonical barycentric-subdivision map $|WS|\\to X$ of [[lem-cg-polyhedral-face-coherence-and-uniform-star-radius]] (i) is a homeomorphism $\\Sigma\\cong X$ that carries the subposet $WS_{\\leq q}$ onto the barycentric subdivision of the cell $C_q$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F16",
          "source": "thm-cg-davis-complex-cell-incidence-and-stabilizers",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Under this identification the cells of $\\Sigma$ are the images of the cells $C_{wW_T}$, of dimension $|T|$; there is one $W$-orbit of cells for each spherical $T$; $\\Sigma$ has finitely many cell shapes and every closed cell meets only finitely many cells; the setwise stabilizer of the cell $wW_T$ is $wW_Tw^{-1}$; and every point of $\\Sigma$ lies in the relative interior of exactly one cell.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "6.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-cell-attachment-by-a-characteristic-map",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The quotient map restricted to $D^n$ is its characteristic map; its image is the closed cell and the image of $\\mathring D^n$ is the open cell.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "$f$ is a **homeomorphism** if $f$ is a continuous bijection whose inverse $f^{-1} : Y \\to X$ is also continuous.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-cw-complex-with-closure-finiteness-and-weak-topology",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **CW complex** is a Hausdorff space $X$ with a filtration $$\\varnothing=X^{-1}\\subseteq X^0\\subseteq X^1\\subseteq\\cdots,\\qquad X=\\bigcup_{n\\geq0}X^n,$$ where $X^0$ is discrete and $X^n$ is obtained from $X^{n-1}$ by attaching a (possibly infinite) family of $n$-disks along maps $S^{n-1}\\to X^{n-1}$ as in [[def-cell-attachment-by-a-characteristic-map]]. The images of the disk interiors are its **open $n$-cells** $e^n_\\alpha$, and the disk maps are their characteristic maps. It moreover satisfies: (C) each closed cell $\\overline {e^n_\\alpha}$ meets only finitely many cells; and (W) $A\\subseteq X$ is closed exactly when $A\\cap\\overline e$ is closed in $\\overline e$ for every cell $e$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-skeleta-cw-subcomplex-and-relative-cw-complex",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Write $X^n$ for the union of cells of dimension at most $n$, with $X^{-1}=\\varnothing$.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-cellular-attachments-with-finite-boundary-support-form-a-cw-complex",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then $Z$, with the old and new cells and their characteristic maps, is a CW complex. The natural inclusions of $A$ and every $Z_k$ are closed embeddings and identify them with subcomplexes. A compatible collection of continuous maps on $A$ and the new characteristic disks defines a continuous map from $Z$ into any space. These conclusions and the construction use no choice principle. The same conclusions hold for a finite number of stages.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1",
            "8.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-cg-spherical-nerve-coset-poset-and-davis-realization",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A subset $T\\subseteq S$ is **spherical** when $W_T$ is finite. Let $\\mathbb S$ denote the set of spherical subsets, partially ordered by inclusion; it has least element $\\emptyset$ because $W_\\emptyset=\\{1\\}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "6.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The canonical homomorphism $W_J^\\ast\\to W_J$, $s\\mapsto s$, is an isomorphism. Hence $(W_J,J)$ is a Coxeter system, its intrinsic length function $\\ell_J$ agrees with the ambient length $\\ell$ on $W_J$, and $W_J\\cap S=J$.",
          "uses": [
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "R:=\\{s^2:s\\in S\\}\\cup\\{(st)^{m(s,t)}:s,t\\in S,\\ m(s,t)<\\infty\\}",
          "uses": [
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "def-cayley-graph",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "with vertex set $G$ and edge set $$E := \\{\\, \\{g,gs\\} : g \\in G,\\ s \\in (S \\cup S^{-1}) \\setminus \\{e\\} \\,\\}.$$",
