    "ex-cg-b2-davis-complex-octagon-and-boundary-circle": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-cg-spherical-nerve-coset-poset-and-davis-realization",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(S,m)$ be a Coxeter matrix with $S$ finite, let $W$ be the presented group with length function $\\ell$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), and for $T\\subseteq S$ let $W_T=\\langle s:s\\in T\\rangle\\le W$ ([[def-cg-parabolic-quotient-and-two-sided-minima]] (1), [[def-generated-subgroup]]); recall $W_T=\\{w\\in W:S(w)\\subseteq T\\}$ for the support $S(w)$ of a reduced expression ([[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (1)).\n\n**(1) Spherical subsets and the nerve.** A subset $T\\subseteq S$ is **spherical** when $W_T$ is finite. Let $\\mathbb S$ denote the set of spherical subsets, partially ordered by inclusion; it has least element $\\emptyset$ because $W_\\emptyset=\\{1\\}$. If $T\\in\\mathbb S$ and $T'\\subseteq T$, then $W_{T'}\\le W_T$ ([[def-generated-subgroup]]), so $T'$ is spherical by [[lem-finite-coset-partition]]: $\\mathbb S$ is downward closed. For each $s\\in S$, the relation $s^2=1$ makes $W_{\\{s\\}}$ finite, so every singleton is spherical ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]). The **nerve** $L$ is the abstract simplicial complex ([[def-abstract-simplicial-complex]]) on vertex set $S$ whose nonempty simplices are the nonempty spherical subsets; it also contains the empty simplex by the library's complex convention. Since $S$ is finite, $L$ is finite.\n\n**(2) The poset of spherical cosets.** For $T\\in\\mathbb S$ and $w\\in W$ let $wW_T$ be the left coset ([[def-coset]]), and put $$WS:=\\{wW_T:w\\in W,\\ T\\in\\mathbb S\\},$$ partially ordered by inclusion of subsets of $W$. For $T=\\emptyset$ this gives $wW_\\emptyset=\\{w\\}$, so $W$ sits inside $WS$ as the set of minimal elements. A member of $WS$ is the resulting subset of $W$, not a choice of representative pair $(w,T)$; the equality, inclusion, and intersection criteria for these cosets are proved in [[lem-cg-spherical-coset-inclusion-and-intersection]].\n\n**(3) The Davis realization.** $\\Sigma:=|WS|$ is the geometric realization of the order complex of the poset $WS$ ([[def-face-poset-and-order-complex]], [[def-geometric-realization-of-an-abstract-simplicial-complex]]): its vertices are the cosets $wW_T$, and its simplices are the finite chains in $WS$. The **chamber** is $K:=|\\mathbb S|$, the order complex of the poset of spherical subsets, and $j\\colon K\\to\\Sigma$ is the simplicial map induced by $T\\mapsto W_T$; this is simplicial because $T\\subseteq T'$ implies $W_T\\subseteq W_{T'}$. As an abstract complex, $K$ is the cone with apex $\\emptyset$ over the barycentric subdivision of $L$, and it is finite, hence compact and Hausdorff ([[prop-a-finite-simplicial-complex-has-compact-hausdorff-realization]]).\n\n**(4) The $W$-action.** Left multiplication $(v,wW_T)\\mapsto (vw)W_T$ is a well-defined left action of $W$ on the set $WS$ by order-preserving bijections ([[def-group]], [[def-coset]]); it induces a simplicial action of $W$ on $\\Sigma$. The **chambers** of $\\Sigma$ are the images $w j(K)=wK$, $w\\in W$, and the map $w\\mapsto wK$ is injective: the vertex $W_\\emptyset=\\{1\\}$ of $K$ is carried to $wW_\\emptyset=\\{w\\}$, and $\\{v\\}=\\{w\\}W_\\emptyset$ forces $v=w$ ([[def-coset]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-cg-spherical-coset-inclusion-and-intersection",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(S,m)$, $W$, $\\ell$, $\\mathbb S$, the parabolics $W_T$ and the poset $WS$ be as in [[def-cg-spherical-nerve-coset-poset-and-davis-realization]]; keep the convention that $S(w)$ is the set of letters of any reduced expression of $w$ ([[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (1)). Let $T,T'\\in\\mathbb S$ and $w,w'\\in W$.\n\n**(1) Equality.** $wW_T=w'W_{T'}$ if and only if $T=T'$ and $w^{-1}w'\\in W_T$. Hence the projection $\\pi\\colon WS\\to\\mathbb S$, $wW_T\\mapsto T$, is well-defined, the members of $WS$ are exactly the left cosets of the subgroups $W_T$ ($T\\in\\mathbb S$), and the left cosets of any one $W_T$ are pairwise disjoint while cosets of distinct parabolics are distinct.\n\n**(2) Inclusion.** $wW_T\\subseteq w'W_{T'}$ if and only if $T\\subseteq T'$ and $w^{-1}w'\\in W_{T'}$ (equivalently $w\\in w'W_{T'}$, equivalently $wW_{T'}=w'W_{T'}$). In particular $W_T\\subseteq W_{T'}$ if and only if $T\\subseteq T'$.\n\n**(3) Intersections are parabolic cosets.** If $wW_T\\cap w'W_{T'}\\neq\\emptyset$, then $$wW_T\\cap w'W_{T'}=uW_{T\\cap T'}\\qquad\\text{for every }u\\in wW_T\\cap w'W_{T'};$$ moreover $wW_T\\cap w'W_{T'}\\neq\\emptyset$ if and only if $w^{-1}w'\\in W_TW_{T'}$, where $W_TW_{T'}=\\{ab:a\\in W_T,\\ b\\in W_{T'}\\}$. Thus the meet of two spherical cosets in the inclusion order, when their intersection is nonempty, is that intersection coset, of type $T\\cap T'$; disjoint spherical cosets have no common lower bound in $WS$.\n\n**(4) The quotient poset.** The left action of (4) of [[def-cg-spherical-nerve-coset-poset-and-davis-realization]] is order-preserving and $\\pi$-invariant, and it is transitive on the cosets of each fixed parabolic; the induced map of posets $W\\backslash WS\\to\\mathbb S$ is an isomorphism. Consequently the action on $WS$ is free on the minimal elements $wW_\\emptyset$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-cg-finite-coxeter-orbit-polytopes-and-face-metrics",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(S,m)$ be a Coxeter matrix with $S$ finite, $W$ its presented group ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]) with Coxeter form $B$ on $V=\\mathbb R^S$ and reflection representation $\\rho$ ([[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]], [[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]]), and let $(d_s)_{s\\in S}$ be positive real numbers. For $T\\in\\mathbb S$ ([[def-cg-spherical-nerve-coset-poset-and-davis-realization]]) put $V_T:=\\operatorname{span}\\{e_s:s\\in T\\}$ ([[def-linear-subspace]], [[def-linear-combination-and-span]]); since $(W_T,T)$ is a Coxeter system of finite type ([[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (2)) with $W_T$ finite, the restriction of $B$ to $V_T$ is positive definite by [[thm-cg-finite-type-positive-definite-criterion]] (1), so $B_T:=B|_{V_T}$ is an inner product ([[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]). Let $v_s^{(T)}\\in V_T$ ($s\\in T$) be its $B$-dual basis, and put $$x_T:=\\sum_{s\\in T}d_s\\,v_s^{(T)}\\in V_T,\\qquad C_T:=\\operatorname{conv}(W_T\\,x_T)\\subseteq V_T.$$\n\n**(1) Cells.** For every spherical $T$, $C_T$ is a compact convex polyhedral cell of dimension $|T|$ inside the Euclidean affine space $(V_T,B_T)$ with $0$ in its interior, a compact convex polyhedral cell in the sense of [[def-finite-convex-cell-complex-and-linear-subdivision]], and its faces are exactly the sets $\\operatorname{conv}(uW_Ux_T)$, $u\\in W_T$, $U\\subseteq T$, each occurring for exactly one coset $uW_U$; face inclusion agrees with coset inclusion (and the simultaneously reversed face and coset orders also agree). This is [[lem-cg-canonical-cell-exposed-faces-and-normal-cones]] applied to the finite-type system $(W_T,T)$ and the point $x_T$ in the open fundamental chamber $\\{v\\in V_T:B(v,e_s)>0\\text{ for all }s\\in T\\}$, whose distances to the simple mirrors are $B(x_T,e_s)=d_s$.\n\n**(2) Projections.** For $U\\subseteq T$, the $B$-orthogonal projection of $x_T$ onto $V_U$ is $x_U$; equivalently $x_T=x_U+z_{T,U}$ with $z_{T,U}\\in V_U^{\\perp}\\cap V_T$, and $z_{T,U}$ is fixed by $\\rho(W_U)$. In particular $x_U$ depends only on $U$ and on the numbers $d_s$ with $s\\in U$.\n\n**(3) Face isometries.** For $U\\subseteq T$ and $w\\in W_T$, the affine map $$\\varphi^{T,U}_w\\colon V_U\\to V_T,\\qquad \\varphi(v)=\\rho(w)(v+z_{T,U}),$$ is a Euclidean isometry of $(V_U,B_U)$ onto the affine span of the face $\\operatorname{conv}(wW_Ux_T)$ and carries $C_U$ onto that face. Hence the intrinsic metric of the face $\\operatorname{conv}(wW_Ux_T)$ of $C_T$ equals that of $C_U$, and depends only on $U$, the numbers $d_s$ ($s\\in U$) and no other choice.\n\n**(4) Cocycle.** For spherical $U\\subseteq T\\subseteq T'$ and $g_1\\in W_T$, $g_2\\in W_{T'}$ one has $$\\varphi^{T',U}_{g_2g_1}=\\varphi^{T',T}_{g_2}\\circ\\varphi^{T,U}_{g_1},$$ an identity of isometries $V_U\\to V_{T'}$; consequently the face identifications of the cells $C_T$ are compatible on common faces and satisfy the cocycle condition of a gluing ([[def-cg-abstract-isometric-polyhedral-gluing-and-chain-metric]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-cg-davis-complex-cell-incidence-and-stabilizers",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(S,m)$ be a Coxeter matrix with $S$ finite, $W$ its presented group with length $\\ell$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), and let $\\mathbb S$, $WS$, $\\Sigma=|WS|$, $K=|\\mathbb S|$, $j\\colon K\\to\\Sigma$ be as in [[def-cg-spherical-nerve-coset-poset-and-davis-realization]]. Let $C_T$, $z_{T,U}$ and $\\varphi^{T,U}_w$ be as in [[lem-cg-finite-coxeter-orbit-polytopes-and-face-metrics]]. For each spherical coset $q=wW_T$, let $\\dot q$ be its unique element of minimum length, which exists by [[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (3). Then:\n\n**(1) The cellulation is an isometric polyhedral gluing.** Adjoining to $WS$ a formal least element $\\varnothing$ corresponding to the empty face gives a poset $P$ whose principal down-sets are finite and are face posets of the compact convex polyhedral cells $C_T$. For each $q=wW_T$, take a copy $C_q$ of $C_T$ with its coordinates in the chart determined by $\\dot q$. For $p=w'W_U\\subseteq q=wW_T$, define the face isometry $h_{p,q}:=\\varphi^{T,U}_{\\dot q^{-1}\\dot p}$ from $C_p$ onto the face of $C_q$ indexed by $p$. These cells and maps form an isometric polyhedral gluing $X$ of shape $P$ in the sense of [[def-cg-abstract-isometric-polyhedral-gluing-and-chain-metric]]: the cocycle condition follows from [[lem-cg-finite-coxeter-orbit-polytopes-and-face-metrics]] (4), and the intersection condition holds because the intersection of two spherical cosets is a spherical coset of type $T\\cap T'$ or empty ([[lem-cg-spherical-coset-inclusion-and-intersection]] (3)), so the images of $C_p$ and $C_q$ meet exactly in the image of the face of $C_{p\\wedge q}$. The standing hypotheses (H1)-(H3) hold: $X$ is connected (it contains the Cayley graph on $W$), locally finite and has finitely many cell shapes (one for each $T\\in\\mathbb S$, and $S$ is finite). Consequently the chain metric $d$ is a metric on $X$ with the weak topology, and $(X,d)$ is complete and proper in the sense of [[def-complete-metric-space]] and [[def-metric-compactness]], by [[thm-cg-polyhedral-chain-metric-topology-and-properness]] (1)-(3); and the canonical barycentric-subdivision map $|WS|\\to X$ of [[lem-cg-polyhedral-face-coherence-and-uniform-star-radius]] (i) is a homeomorphism $\\Sigma\\cong X$ that carries the subposet $WS_{\\leq q}$ onto the barycentric subdivision of the cell $C_q$.\n\n**(2) Cells, incidence and stabilizers.** Under this identification the cells of $\\Sigma$ are the images of the cells $C_{wW_T}$, of dimension $|T|$; there is one $W$-orbit of cells for each spherical $T$; $\\Sigma$ has finitely many cell shapes and every closed cell meets only finitely many cells; the setwise stabilizer of the cell $wW_T$ is $wW_Tw^{-1}$; and every point of $\\Sigma$ lies in the relative interior of exactly one cell. For $q=wW_T$, use the chart fixed by its unique minimum-length representative $\\dot q$; if a point $y$ in the relative interior of that cell corresponds to $y'\\in C_T$ and $y'$ lies in the relative interior of the chamber face $w_0C^T_I$ of the finite-type chamber decomposition of $V_T$ (where $w_0\\in W_T$, $I\\subseteq T$, $B(w_0^{-1}y',e_s)=0$ for $s\\in I$ and $B(w_0^{-1}y',e_s)>0$ for $s\\in T\\setminus I$), then $$\\operatorname{Stab}_W(y)=(\\dot q w_0)W_I(\\dot q w_0)^{-1},$$ a conjugate of the spherical parabolic $W_I$. In particular every point stabilizer is finite, is a spherical parabolic, and is contained in the setwise stabilizer $wW_Tw^{-1}$ of its cell. The relative interiors of the cells partition $\\Sigma$.\n\n**(3) The action is proper.** The $W$-action on $\\Sigma$ is cellular and isometric for $d$, and it is proper: for every compact subset $C\\subseteq\\Sigma$ the set $\\{w\\in W:wC\\cap C\\ne\\emptyset\\}$ is finite.\n\n**(4) Compact chamber quotient.** Give $W\\backslash\\Sigma$ the quotient topology of the orbit projection $\\Sigma\\to W\\backslash\\Sigma$ ([[def-quotient-topology]]). The projection $WS\\to\\mathbb S$ induces a $W$-invariant continuous map $p\\colon\\Sigma\\to K$ that is the identity on the chamber and maps every translated chamber simplex back to its simplex of $K$; passing to quotients gives a continuous bijection $\\bar p\\colon W\\backslash\\Sigma\\to K$, and $W\\backslash\\Sigma$ is compact as the image of the compact chamber $K$ under the quotient map, so $\\bar p$ is a homeomorphism ([[thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps]] (1),(3)). In particular $W\\backslash\\Sigma$ is compact, and $K$, the chamber, is a strict fundamental domain for the action.\n\n**(5) The model $U(W,K)$.** Give $W$ the discrete topology, $W\\times K$ the product topology, and $U(W,K):=(W\\times K)/\\!\\sim$ the quotient topology ([[def-standard-topologies]], [[def-product-topology]], [[def-quotient-topology]]), where $(w,x)\\sim(w',x')$ iff $x=x'$ and $w^{-1}w'$ lies in the subgroup generated by $S(x):=\\{s\\in S:x\\in K_s\\}$ with $K_s:=|\\mathbb S_{\\geq\\{s\\}}|$ ([[def-generated-subgroup]]). Then $[w,x]\\mapsto w\\cdot j(x)$ is a well-defined $W$-equivariant homeomorphism $U(W,K)\\to\\Sigma$ with inverse given by the carrier-simplex coordinates of [[def-cg-spherical-nerve-coset-poset-and-davis-realization]]: a point of $\\Sigma$ lies in a unique simplex, a chain $w_0W_{T_0}\\subset\\cdots\\subset w_kW_{T_k}$, and by [[lem-cg-spherical-coset-inclusion-and-intersection]] (2) the chain equals $w_0W_{T_0}\\subset\\cdots\\subset w_0W_{T_k}$, so the barycentric coordinates define a point of the simplex of $K$ on $T_0\\subset\\cdots\\subset T_k$; the two maps are mutually inverse by construction and continuous for the stated quotient and weak topologies.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-cg-davis-cellulation-cw-structure-and-cayley-skeleta",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(S,m)$ be a Coxeter matrix with $S$ finite, $W$ its presented group, and let $\\Sigma$ carry the cellulation of [[thm-cg-davis-complex-cell-incidence-and-stabilizers]] with cells the spherical cosets $wW_T$, $T\\in\\mathbb S$ ([[def-cg-spherical-nerve-coset-poset-and-davis-realization]]); write $\\mathbb S$ for the spherical subsets and $C_T$ for the Coxeter cells of [[lem-cg-finite-coxeter-orbit-polytopes-and-face-metrics]]. Then:\n\n**(1) The cells are disks.** For $T=\\emptyset$, $C_T=\\{0\\}$ is the closed zero-ball. For nonempty $T$, the cell $C_T$ is homeomorphic to the closed disk $\\overline B(0,1)\\subseteq V_T$ by the radial map $$\\psi\\colon C_T\\to\\overline B(0,1),\\qquad \\psi(0)=0,\\quad \\psi(x)=\\frac{x}{t_{\\max}(x/\\|x\\|_B)}\\ (x\\ne0),$$ where $t_{\\max}(u)=\\min\\{\\ell_i(0)/(\\ell_i(0)-\\ell_i(u)):\\ell_i(u)<\\ell_i(0)\\}$ is the exit parameter of the unit ray through $u$ for a finite list of affine functions $\\ell_i\\ge0$ defining $C_T=\\{v:\\ell_i(v)\\ge0\\}$ with $\\ell_i(0)>0$; $\\psi$ carries the boundary $\\partial C_T$ onto the unit sphere. Consequently the cells $wW_T$ admit characteristic maps from closed $|T|$-disks ([[def-cell-attachment-by-a-characteristic-map]]).\n\n**(2) CW structure.** With these characteristic maps and the face-identification attaching maps, the cellulation is a CW complex in the sense of [[def-cw-complex-with-closure-finiteness-and-weak-topology]]: the weak topology is the topology of the gluing of [[thm-cg-davis-complex-cell-incidence-and-stabilizers]], and the cells meeting the closed cell $wW_T$ are the cells $vW_V$ with $vW_V\\cap wW_T\\ne\\emptyset$, equivalently $v\\in wW_TW_V$; by [[lem-cg-spherical-coset-inclusion-and-intersection]] (3) and the finiteness of the spherical subsets $V$ and of both $W_T$ and $W_V$ these are finitely many, so the closure finiteness condition (C) holds.\n\n**(3) Skeleta.** The skeleta ([[def-skeleta-cw-subcomplex-and-relative-cw-complex]]) are: $\\Sigma^0=W$, the cosets $wW_\\emptyset=\\{w\\}$; $\\Sigma^1$ is the (undirected, $S$-labelled) Cayley graph of $(W,S)$ ([[def-cayley-graph]], [[def-directed-labelled-cayley-graph]]), each $1$-cell $wW_{\\{s\\}}=\\{w,ws\\}$ being an edge labelled $s$; and $\\Sigma^2$ is Davis's reduced Cayley $2$-complex of the Coxeter presentation $W=\\langle S\\mid s^2\\ (s\\in S),\\ (st)^{m(s,t)}\\ (s\\ne t,\\ m(s,t)<\\infty)\\rangle$: the involution relators $s^2$ contribute only edge backtracks, with no $2$-cells, and the finite pair-relator circuits are identified up to cyclic shift and reversal; its $2$-cells are the cosets $wW_{\\{s,t\\}}$ with $m(s,t)<\\infty$, each a $2m(s,t)$-gon whose boundary closed edge path is $w,ws,wst,\\dots,w(st)^{m(s,t)}=w$.\n\n**(4) Two-dimensional case.** For $|T|=2$, $C_T$ is the regular $2m(s,t)$-gon when $d_s=d_t$, and for $|T|=1$, $C_T$ is the interval from $-d_se_s$ to $d_se_s$; the cellulation has no cells of dimension $\\ge3$ exactly when no three-element spherical subset exists.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-cg-finite-chamber-tiling-and-coset-face-identification",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(3) The face identification and the stabilisers.** The assignment\n$wW_I\\mapsto w\\overline{C_I}$ is a well-defined bijection from the coset face poset onto the\nset of proper faces $\\{w\\overline{C_I}:w\\in W,\\ I\\subsetneq S\\}$ (the remaining sets\n$w\\overline{C_S}$ are all equal to $\\{0\\}$, the common face of all chambers), and for all\n$w,v\\in W$ and $I,J\\subseteq S$\n$$wW_I=vW_J\\iff w\\overline{C_I}=v\\overline{C_J},\\qquad wW_I\\subseteq vW_J\\iff w\\overline{C_J}\\subseteq v\\overline{C_I},$$\n$$w\\overline{C_I}\\cap v\\overline{C_J}=w\\overline{C_{I\\cup J\\cup S(v^{-1}w)}},\\qquad wC_I\\cap vC_J\\neq\\emptyset\\iff wW_I=vW_J,$$\nso the relative interiors of the faces partition $V$; moreover\n$V\\setminus\\{0\\}=\\bigsqcup wC_I$, the disjoint union running over the cosets $wW_I$ with\n$I\\subsetneq S$. For every $x\\in wC_I$ one has\n$\\operatorname{Stab}_W(x)=wW_Iw^{-1}$, and the setwise stabiliser\n$\\{w'\\in W:w'w\\overline{C_I}=w\\overline{C_I}\\}$ of the face $w\\overline{C_I}$ is the same\nsubgroup $wW_Iw^{-1}$.\n\n**(4) The spherical triangulation.** Assume $n\\ge1$. Then\n$S^{n-1}=\\bigsqcup(wC_I\\cap S^{n-1})$, the union running over the cosets $wW_I$ with\n$I\\subsetneq S$, and\n$$\\Sigma:=\\{w\\overline{C_I}\\cap S^{n-1}:w\\in W,\\ I\\subsetneq S\\}$$\nis a finite simplicial complex in the sphere: each member is the spherical simplex whose\nvertices are the points $\\rho(w)v_s/\\lVert v_s\\rVert_B$ $(s\\notin I)$ in the same sense, the relative interiors\n$wC_I\\cap S^{n-1}$ are pairwise disjoint and cover $S^{n-1}$, and the intersection of two\nmembers is a common face by (3); its face poset is isomorphic to the coset face poset by\n$wW_I\\mapsto w\\overline{C_I}\\cap S^{n-1}$. Consequently the abstract simplicial complex $K$\nwith vertices the cosets $wW_{S\\setminus\\{s\\}}$ $(w\\in W,\\ s\\in S)$ and simplices the sets\n$\\{wW_{S\\setminus\\{s\\}}:s\\notin I\\}$ $(w\\in W,\\ I\\subsetneq S)$ is a triangulation of\n$S^{n-1}$: the simplexwise affine map $\\varphi:|K|\\to S^{n-1}$ that sends the barycentric\ncoordinate at the vertex $wW_{S\\setminus\\{s\\}}$ to the direction of $\\rho(w)v_s$ is a\ncontinuous bijection, $|K|$ is compact because $K$ is finite\n([[prop-a-finite-simplicial-complex-has-compact-hausdorff-realization]]) and $S^{n-1}$ is\nHausdorff, so $\\varphi$ is a homeomorphism\n([[def-geometric-realization-of-an-abstract-simplicial-complex]],\n[[thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps]], [[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]]). In\nparticular $K$ has exactly $|W|$ maximal simplices, indexed by the chambers. For $S=\\emptyset$\nthe group is trivial, $V=0$, and $S^{-1}=\\emptyset$ is triangulated by the empty complex.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**The presented group.** Let $R\\subseteq F(S)$ be the set of relators\n$$R:=\\{s^2:s\\in S\\}\\cup\\{(st)^{m(s,t)}:s,t\\in S,\\ m(s,t)<\\infty\\},$$\nwhere $F(S)$ is the free group on $S$ ([[def-free-group]], [[thm-reduced-words-form-the-free-group]]) and $(st)^k$ is the $k$-fold product ([[def-group-power]]), and let $N:=\\langle\\!\\langle R\\rangle\\!\\rangle_{F(S)}$ be the normal closure of $R$ in $F(S)$ ([[def-normal-closure]], [[prop-normal-closure-is-products-of-conjugates]]). Define\n$$W:=F(S)/N,$$\nand write $s$ (as well as $sN$) for the image of $s\\in S$ in $W$ ([[def-quotient-group]], [[def-group]]).\n\n**Universal property.** For every group $G$ and every map $f:S\\to G$ with $f(s)^2=1$ for all $s\\in S$ and $(f(s)f(t))^{m(s,t)}=1$ in $G$ for all $s\\ne t$ with $m(s,t)<\\infty$, there is a unique group homomorphism $\\varphi:W\\to G$ with $\\varphi(s)=f(s)$ for all $s\\in S$ ([[def-group-homomorphism]], [[thm-quotient-group-universal-property]], [[thm-von-dyck]], [[def-group-presentation]], [[def-relators-relations-and-finite-presentations]], [[prop-equality-of-words-in-a-presentation]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-lagrange",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$$|G|=[G:H]\\,|H|.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "external_source_quotes": [
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "M. W. Davis, The Geometry and Topology of Coxeter Groups",
          "source_section": "Example 3.1.6, printed p. 28",
          "quote": "there is a surjection W → D_m",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ],
          "note": "The item independently proves the m=4 order by normal forms and an explicit square-reflection model."
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "M. W. Davis, The Geometry and Topology of Coxeter Groups",
          "source_section": "Example 7.3.2(ii), printed p. 129",
          "quote": "regular 2m-gon if x is equidistant from the two rays",
          "uses": [
