          "note": "The item locally counts the eight faces; this excerpt corroborates regularity in the equal-distance case."
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "The Coxeter presentation reduces every word to one of $r^k$ or $r^ks$ for $k=0,1,2,3$; an explicit square-reflection representation separates the eight forms and proves $|W|=8$ with both singleton parabolics of order two. Thus all four parabolics are finite, Lagrange gives four cosets of each singleton type, and A1/A5 give eight vertices, four edges of each label and one top cell, for 17 cells.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "A4 face incidence and Step 1.1 give eight vertices and eight edges, hence an octagon; A2 shows the incident edges at each vertex have the two distinct singleton types, so labels alternate. A6 proves regularity when distances agree. A5 identifies the Davis realization with the barycentric subdivision of this cell and gives its disk topology; the finite chamber theorem identifies the reversed-incidence boundary cycle with the rank-two Coxeter complex.",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.2",
          "step": "2.2",
          "claim": "The spherical-subset poset has exactly two maximal chains, producing the two chamber triangles along their common diagonal and hence the square K; A5 gives the compact quotient W\\backslash\\Sigma\\cong K. Every group element lies in one edge of each singleton type, so the eight vertices give sixteen maximal coset chains, two per chamber.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Each octagon edge is isometric to its rank-one cell by A4 and has length $2d_s$ or $2d_t$ by A6. Alternating labels make equal distances give equal sides and, by A6, the regular case; unequal distances give unequal sides and exclude regularity. Face counts and disk topology do not depend on the positive distance values, and the finite argument uses no Choice.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
